@page "/docs/utilities/scrollbar"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Scrollbar
<PageTitle>Scrollbar - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Scrollbar</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Utilities</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">CSS only</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>A slim, theme-aware scrollbar in place of the chunky OS one: a transparent track and a rounded pill thumb that reads as a hairline at rest and firms up on hover. The colours come from the same tokens as everything else, so it tracks light and dark and every preset. Components already mark their scroll areas with <code>scrollbar-thin</code> - but the mark is inert until your project opts in, so an app that never asked keeps its native bars everywhere.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(ScrollbarUsageExample)"
                Setup="@null"
                Description="A class on the overflow container. The hover, activity, none and auto utilities come with the sheet Blaizio already imports into your Tailwind entry point; scrollbar-thin additionally needs the project-level opt-in below." />

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Turning it on</h2>
        <p>Pass <code>--scrollbar</code> to <code>blaizio new</code>, <code>blaizio add</code> or <code>blaizio apply</code>, and the CLI appends this block to your Tailwind input - or paste it yourself. It redefines the stub that ships in <code>blaizio.css</code>, and every <code>scrollbar-thin</code> mark in the components lights up at once. The block lands in your file, so restyling the bar is an edit and opting back out is a delete.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@OptInCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>This site opts in the same way - the block sits in the docs' own <code>app.css</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Variants"
          Description="How present the bar is: always, only under the pointer, or only while the box is moving. The last one hides it entirely, which suits a list that scrolls through controls of its own."
          Example="typeof(ScrollbarVariantsExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Overriding a component's bar"
          Description="Components that ship a scrollbar (select and command lists, menu contents, the virtualizer's viewport, a scrollable table) accept the override through their Class. Every scrollbar-* utility is one class group, so what you pass replaces the default instead of fighting it - which is also the way in for a scrollbar of your own: pass scrollbar-auto alongside your own class and there is nothing of ours left to beat."
          Example="typeof(ScrollbarOverrideExample)" />

    <section class="space-y-6">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Class Reference</h2>
        <ClassTable Rows="@Classes" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Notes</h2>
        <p><code>scrollbar-activity</code> needs one thing from the host: an <code>is-scrolling</code> class on the container while it is being scrolled. A few lines of your own scroll handler, or a delegated listener for a data attribute across the whole app, as this site does.</p>
        <p>Firefox styles the bar through <code>scrollbar-width</code> and <code>scrollbar-color</code>; Chromium and WebKit get the <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code> pseudo-elements, where the hover step lives. Both look right, the hover refinement is a progressive enhancement.</p>
        <p><code>scrollbar-auto</code> deliberately declares nothing for <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code>. A browser paints its own bar only where no author rule has claimed those pseudo-elements, so handing it back means leaving them alone rather than resetting them - a reset renders an invisible bar in a reserved gutter.</p>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private const string OptInCss =
        """
        @utility scrollbar-thin {
          scrollbar-width: thin;
          scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent;

          &::-webkit-scrollbar {
            width: 8px;
            height: 8px;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
            background: transparent;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
            border-radius: 9999px;
            background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground));
            border: 2px solid transparent;
            background-clip: padding-box;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
            background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
          }
        }
        """;

    private static readonly (string, string[], string)[] Classes =
    [
        ("scrollbar-thin",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent;",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; height: 8px; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { border-radius: 9999px; background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); border: 2px solid transparent; background-clip: padding-box; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover { background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground); }",
        ],
        "A slim themed bar, always visible while the content overflows. Project-level opt-in: inert until the block above is in your input."),

        ("scrollbar-hover",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: transparent transparent;",
            "&:hover { scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; height: 8px; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { border-radius: 9999px; background-color: transparent; border: 2px solid transparent; background-clip: padding-box; }",
            "&:hover::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); }",
        ],
        "The same bar, revealed only while the pointer is over the container. The gutter stays reserved, so nothing shifts."),

        ("scrollbar-activity",
        [
            "color-scheme: light dark;",
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: transparent transparent;",
            "&.is-scrolling { scrollbar-color: var(--color-muted-foreground) transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: transparent; transition: background-color 0.4s ease; }",
            "&.is-scrolling::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground); }",
        ],
        "Revealed only while the container is being scrolled, fading out shortly after. Needs an is-scrolling class from the host."),

        ("scrollbar-none",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: none;",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }",
        ],
        "No bar at all. The content still scrolls, by wheel, touch and keyboard."),

        ("scrollbar-auto",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: auto;",
            "scrollbar-color: auto;",
        ],
        "The browser's own bar. The opt-out, and the clean slate for a bar of your own. It claims no ::-webkit-scrollbar rules, which is what lets the browser paint its default."),
    ];
}
